## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates practical memory allocator design for high-throughput systems where NUMA locality and lock-free reclamation are critical—common requirements in databases, analytics platforms, and low-latency trading systems.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA-aware allocation**: Assign per-thread caches bound to specific NUMA nodes; central pool maintains node-local segments to minimize cross-node memory accesses
- **Lock-free per-thread caches**: Thread-local freelists eliminate contention entirely for the fast path, with batch fetching from central pool only on cache misses
- **Hazard pointers vs epoch-based reclamation**: Hazard pointers provide precise,ABA-free reclamation with bounded memory overhead, ideal for read-heavy workloads; epoch-based reclamation offers simpler implementation but may retain memory longer
- **Cache-line alignment**: Align blocks to cache line boundaries (typically 64B) and include padding to prevent false sharing between threads
- **Batch refills and backpressure**: Fetching multiple blocks per central pool access amortizes synchronization cost; capacity limits on thread-local caches prevent unbounded memory growth

